// Expected: c0 at one session and at two.
//
// The same shape as cap_forgeable_is_not_a_reuse_pair.vp with a generated
// nonce that travels guarded. A guard only takes capability away from the
// attacker, so it can never turn a query that holds into one that fails, and
// the unguarded spelling of this model holds.
//
// It did not. Guarding the nonce removed the substitution the search would
// otherwise have made on it, and the search fell back to minting a ciphertext
// under the declared `forgeable` assumption and pairing that mint with
// Alice's own. The verdict moved from c0 to c1 on the strength of one pair of
// brackets, which is the shortest proof that the pairing was wrong.

attacker[active]

principal Alice[
	knows private k
	knows private m
	knows public ad
	generates n_e
	e = AEAD_ENC[forgeable](k, n_e, m, ad)
]

Alice -> Bob: [n_e], e

principal Bob[
	knows private k
	knows public ad
	d = AEAD_DEC(k, n_e, e, ad)?
	_ = HASH(d)
]

queries[
	confidentiality? m
]
